</figure>
<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All evaluations are done using the <span id="A3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Olmes</span> evaluation standard introduced by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib107" title="" class="ltx_ref">107</a>]</cite>, following key metrics from the <span id="A3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OLMo 2</span> framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">68</a>]</cite>. Table <a href="#A3.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ Appendix C Evaluation Details ‣ FlexOlmo: Open Language Models for Flexible Data Use" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> presents a detailed breakdown of our evaluation datasets and metrics. General description is provided in §<a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Evaluation ‣ 4 Experimental Setup ‣ FlexOlmo: Open Language Models for Flexible Data Use" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>; here, we provide more details.</p>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">SciRiFF</h5>

<div id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We select five subtasks that do not require structured prediction: <span id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">BioASQ-Factoid</span>, <span id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">BioASQ-Yes/No</span>, <span id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">BioASQ-General</span>, <span id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PubMedQA</span>, and <span id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">COVID-QA</span>. , with our tables reporting the average performance across all five tasks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">MATH</h5>

<div id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We assess solution correctness through exact matching with ground truth answers, following the methodology established in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib108" title="" class="ltx_ref">108</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">News Generation</h5>

<div id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use the <span id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">muse-bench/MUSE-News</span> dataset from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib109" title="" class="ltx_ref">109</a>]</cite>, selecting articles containing between 64 and 128 tokens. Models are prompted to continue an article given a prefix of the first 32 tokens, using a sampling temperature of 0.8. A <span id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama-3.3-70B-Instruct</span> model serves as the judge, evaluating each completion based on journalistic quality (2 points), topical coherence (2 points), and clarity/fluency (1 point), for a total score between 0 and 5, which is then normalized to a 0–100 scale. To reduce variance, we generate five completions per prompt and report the average score.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Poem Generation</h5>

<div id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We employ the <span id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">merve/poetry</span> dataset<span id="footnote7" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">7</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">7</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">7</span>
              
              
              
            <a href="https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/ishnoor/poetry-analysis-with-machine-learning/data" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/ishnoor/poetry-analysis-with-machine-learning/data</a></span></span></span>, filtering poems to those containing 64–128 tokens. From a total of 176 poems (147 Renaissance, 29 Modern), we reserve five poems (three Renaissance, two Modern) for few-shot examples and use 100 for testing. Models continue each poem from its first four lines, following the same prompting and evaluation settings as in <span id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">NewsGen</span>. A genre-aware <span id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama-3.3-70B-Instruct</span> judge evaluates each completion based on poetic craftsmanship (2 points), thematic coherence (2 points), and clarity/fluency (1 point), with scores normalized to a 0–100 scale. As with news generation, five completions are generated per prompt, and we report the average score across all instances.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Methodology Intuition</h2>

<section id="A4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.1 </span>Problem Setup</h3>

<div id="A4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given an input data <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>h</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{h}</annotation></semantics></math>, the router learning can be viewed as a multi-class classification problem with <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+1</annotation></semantics></math> classes: a public class <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{\text{pub}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mtext>pub</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{\text{pub}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> closed classes <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathcal{C}_{i}\}_{i=1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathcal{C}_{i}\}_{i=1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The scoring function <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}:\mathbb{R}^{h}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>h</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}:\mathbb{R}^{h}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}(\mathbf{x})=\mathbf{r}_{i}\cdot\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐫</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}(\mathbf{x})=\mathbf{r}_{i}\cdot\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the score for class <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐫</mi><mi>𝐢</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the router embedding and the higher scores indicate higher class membership likelihood.</p>
</div>
<section id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Training: Pairwise Binary Classification</h5>

<div id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In training experts to coordinate (§<a href="#S3.SS3.SSS1" title="3.3.1 Training Experts to Coordinate ‣ 3.3 Training Algorithm ‣ 3 FlexOlmo: LMs with Flexible Data Use ‣ FlexOlmo: Open Language Models for Flexible Data Use" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3.1</span></a>), we learn <math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> binary classifiers <math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{f_{i}\}_{i=1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{f_{i}\}_{i=1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, where each <math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}:\mathbb{R}^{h}\to\{\mathcal{C}_{\text{pub}},\mathcal{C}_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>h</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mtext>pub</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}:\mathbb{R}^{h}\to\{\mathcal{C}_{\text{pub}},\mathcal{C}_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math> discriminates between <math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{\text{pub}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mtext>pub</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{\text{pub}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A4.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}(\mathbf{x})=\begin{cases}\mathcal{C}_{i}&amp;\text{if }s_{i}(\mathbf{x})&gt;s_{\text{pub}}(\mathbf{x})\\
\mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{pub}}&amp;\text{otherwise}.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mtext>pub</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>pub</mi></msub></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}(\mathbf{x})=\begin{cases}\mathcal{C}_{i}&amp;\text{if }s_{i}(\mathbf{x})&gt;s_{\text{pub}}(\mathbf{x})\\
\mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{pub}}&amp;\text{otherwise}.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The decision boundary <math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> between <math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{pub}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>pub</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{pub}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as:</p>
<table id="A4.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{i}:=\{\mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{h}:s_{\mathrm{pub}}(\mathbf{x})=s_{i}(\mathbf{x})\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>h</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>pub</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{i}:=\{\mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{h}:s_{\mathrm{pub}}(\mathbf{x})=s_{i}(\mathbf{x})\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Inference: Multiclass Classification</h5>

<div id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">At inference time (§ <a href="#S3.SS3.SSS2" title="3.3.2 Domain-Informed Router ‣ 3.3 Training Algorithm ‣ 3 FlexOlmo: LMs with Flexible Data Use ‣ FlexOlmo: Open Language Models for Flexible Data Use" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3.2</span></a>), these <math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> binary classifiers are combined into a unified multi-class classifier that maps from <math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>h</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{h}</annotation></semantics></math> to the complete class space <math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}_{\text{pub}},\mathcal{C}_{1},\ldots,\mathcal{C}_{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mtext>pub</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}_{\text{pub}},\mathcal{C}_{1},\ldots,\mathcal{C}_{n}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The final classification decision is determined by:</p>
<table id="A4.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(\mathbf{x})=\arg\max_{i\in\{\mathrm{pub},1,\cdots,n\}}s_{i}(\mathbf{x})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>pub</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></munder></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(\mathbf{x})=\arg\max_{i\in\{\mathrm{pub},1,\cdots,n\}}s_{i}(\mathbf{x}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The key question is: how could we make the ensemble of binary classifiers <math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{f_{i}\}_{i=1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{f_{i}\}_{i=1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> route inputs as close to if we had trained one unified multiclass classifier <math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> end-to-end on all data?</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our intuition is if each binary classifier <math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> learns the decision boundary "<math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> vs. not <math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>" rather than just "<math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{\text{pub}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mtext>pub</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{\text{pub}}</annotation></semantics></math>", this could make decision boundary that tightly encircles <math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> which leads to better multi-class classification.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.2 </span>Anchor Point and Negative Bias</h3>

<figure id="A4.F6" class="ltx_figure" style="width:44%;"><img src="2507.07024v1/figures/negative_bias.png" id="A4.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:204/145;" width="204" height="145" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A4.F6.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 6</span>: </span><span id="A4.F6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">During training, the negative bias shifts the decision boundary. So that a more selective subset of data will be used to train the expert corresponding to <math id="A4.F6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To ensure each classifier <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> learns a better decision boundary <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, we propose two key techniques:</p>
</div>
<section id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Freezing <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\mathrm{pub}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>pub</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\mathrm{pub}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\mathrm{pub}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>pub</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\mathrm{pub}}</annotation></semantics></math> as anchor points</h5>

<div id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">During training of each binary classifier <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, we fix the public expert <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\mathrm{pub}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>pub</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\mathrm{pub}}</annotation></semantics></math> and router embedding <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}_{\mathrm{pub}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐫</mi><mi>pub</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}_{\mathrm{pub}}</annotation></semantics></math> to ensure all classifiers share a common coordinate system by maintaining:</p>
<table id="A4.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\mathrm{pub}}(\mathbf{x})=\mathbf{r}_{\mathrm{pub}}\cdot\mathbf{x},\forall i\in\{1,\cdots,n\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>pub</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐫</mi><mi>pub</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\mathrm{pub}}(\mathbf{x})=\mathbf{r}_{\mathrm{pub}}\cdot\mathbf{x},\forall i\in\{1,\cdots,n\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Without this constraint, each binary classifier would optimize both <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}_{\mathrm{pub}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐫</mi><mi>pub</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}_{\mathrm{pub}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐫</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> independently, resulting in inconsistent coordinate systems where <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}_{\mathrm{pub}}^{(i)}\neq\mathbf{r}_{\mathrm{pub}}^{(j)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐫</mi><mi>pub</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>≠</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐫</mi><mi>pub</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}_{\mathrm{pub}}^{(i)}\neq\mathbf{r}_{\mathrm{pub}}^{(j)}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\neq j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\neq j</annotation></semantics></math> during the training of binary classifiers <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Such inconsistency would invalidate the multi-class classifier <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math>, as it would attempt to compare scores computed in incompatible embedding spaces. By maintaining a fixed reference point, we ensure all decision boundaries <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are defined relative to the same coordinate system, enabling their meaningful composition during inference without additional training.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Adding a negative bias</h5>

<div id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">During training of <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, each binary classifier only needs to satisfy <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}_{i}\cdot\mathbf{x}&gt;\mathbf{r}_{\mathrm{pub}}\cdot\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐫</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐫</mi><mi>pub</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}_{i}\cdot\mathbf{x}&gt;\mathbf{r}_{\mathrm{pub}}\cdot\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}\in\mathcal{C}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}\in\mathcal{C}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
When <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{pub}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>pub</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{pub}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is separable, many possible <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐫</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> vectors (no bias boundary in <a href="#A4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ D.2 Anchor Point and Negative Bias ‣ Appendix D Methodology Intuition ‣ FlexOlmo: Open Language Models for Flexible Data Use" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 6</span></a>) can satisfy this constraint without precisely characterizing the specialized region of <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We refine this by adding a negative bias term to each expert:</p>
<table id="A4.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}_{i}\cdot\mathbf{x}+b_{i}&gt;\mathbf{r}_{\text{pub}}\cdot\mathbf{x}\quad\forall i\in\{1,2,...,n\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐫</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐫</mi><mtext>pub</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}_{i}\cdot\mathbf{x}+b_{i}&gt;\mathbf{r}_{\text{pub}}\cdot\mathbf{x}\quad\forall i\in\{1,2,...,n\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">As <a href="#A4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ D.2 Anchor Point and Negative Bias ‣ Appendix D Methodology Intuition ‣ FlexOlmo: Open Language Models for Flexible Data Use" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 6</span></a> illustrates, the negative bias term could help move the decision boundary <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> closer to <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>’s data points.
This means, during training, a more selective subset of data will be used to train the local expert. This facilitates merging, where experts compete not only with <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{pub}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>pub</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{pub}}</annotation></semantics></math> but with each other.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
</article>
</div>
</div>
